FFmpeg 9.0.2's HLS demuxer does not take the timestamps of a stream that restarts or jumps at an EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY
(it does not read the tag; the format is only flagged AVFMT_TS_DISCONT, so the user of the demuxer is to cope). An
advertisement or a splice often starts its segments again from zero, or from another base: the packets then meet a
player's clock far from where the playlist puts them, and are dropped or waited for, and a seek past the first such
segment never reaches the cutoff hls_read_packet drops packets up to (the playlist's start of the segment, on the
timeline of the first segment's timestamps) and ends reading the playlist again from its start.

The segments after a discontinuity have their packets moved onto the playlist's timeline: by the difference of where the
playlist's durations put the segment (the start of the timeline, c->first_timestamp, plus the playlist's offset to it,
plus the durations before the segment) and the timestamp of the segment's first packet. One offset per playlist, not per
stream, so audio and video of a segment stay together. It is taken
 - at the first packet after a seek, whatever segment the seek landed in;
 - at the first packet of a playlist taken up (a variant switch, a rendition), which has the offset of the playlists read
   before it when it has none: the variants of a stream share the baseline of their timestamps;
 - when reading on, at the first packet after a segment that follows a discontinuity whose timestamp jumps by more than 2
   seconds against the last packet of its own stream (the packets of the segment before may still come out of the
   subdemuxer after the segment changed, the h264 parser holding the last pictures, so the jump tells which packet is
   the first). Left untaken once reading has gone past the next segment with no jump (a server that keeps its timestamps).
A difference of less than half a target duration is not taken (a server that keeps its timestamps across the
discontinuity), a packet that the offset would put outside the playlist's timeline keeps its own timestamps (a late packet
of the segment before), and where the playlist puts the segment is not known the offset stays as it is. A break that
restarts the timestamps by less than 2 seconds is not seen: it is not worth seeing, the error is that small. A playlist
without the tag is unchanged: the code does nothing until a segment follows one.

Restricted to libavformat/hls.c. Applied by scripts/build_dependencies.sh to FFmpeg 9.0.2, after ffmpeg-hls-live-seek.patch.
build_dependencies.sh also sets --extra-version=nits-hls-timeline, which the tests read to tell this build from another.

